  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Four kilometres under Antarctic ice, a lake never froze. Vostok runs 250 kilometres long, fifty wide, and up to 900 metres deep.",
        "visual_query": "antarctic ice sheet vast plateau",
        "visual_fallback": "antarctic ice"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Its water sits near minus three degrees and stays liquid anyway, squeezed by hundreds of bars of pressure from the ice overhead.",
        "visual_query": "deep blue glacial ice crevasse",
        "visual_fallback": "glacial ice"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It has been sealed off from the sky for roughly fifteen million years. So what has been living down there all that time?",
        "visual_query": "antarctic research station snow wind",
        "visual_fallback": "polar station"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Russian drillers reached the water in February 2012, nearly 3,770 metres down, through a borehole held open with kerosene and Freon.",
        "visual_query": "ice core drilling antarctica equipment",
        "visual_fallback": "ice drilling"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That was the problem. By May 2017 the Russian team reported their water samples were too contaminated to say anything reliable about the lake.",
        "visual_query": "scientist handling ice core sample",
        "visual_fallback": "ice core"
      }
    ],
